### Manual API walkthrough for IntelliJ HTTP Client (contains NO secrets).
### Run the requests top to bottom while the app is running on localhost:8080.
### Two separate users are used (A = you, B = someone else) to demonstrate ownership (404).

### ===== USER A =====

### 1. Register user A (unique email each run) -> capture the exact email used
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{"name":"Ankit","email":"ankit{{$timestamp}}@example.com","password":"SecurePassword123"}

> {% client.global.set("emailA", response.body.email); %}

### 2. Log in as user A -> capture the JWT for every later request
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"{{emailA}}","password":"SecurePassword123"}

> {% client.global.set("tokenA", response.body.token); %}

### 3. Create a savings account for user A (owner comes from the token, not the body)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"accountType":"SAVINGS"}

> {% client.global.set("accountIdA", response.body.id); %}

### 4. List MY accounts (always the caller's own list)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

### 5. Deposit 1000.00 -> 201, balanceAfter 1000.00
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/deposit
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":1000.00,"description":"initial deposit"}

### 6. Withdraw 300.00 -> 201, balanceAfter 700.00
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/withdraw
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":300.00,"description":"rent"}

### 7. Withdraw too much -> 422 Insufficient funds
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/withdraw
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":5000.00}

### 8. Invalid amount -> 400 with fieldErrors.amount
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/deposit
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":-10}

### 9. Check the balance -> 700.00
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

### 10. Transaction history -> both entries, newest first
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}

### ===== USER B (to demonstrate ownership = 404, not 403) =====

### 11. Register user B -> capture the exact email used
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{"name":"Someone Else","email":"other{{$timestamp}}@example.com","password":"SecurePassword123"}

> {% client.global.set("emailB", response.body.email); %}

### 12. Log in as user B
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"{{emailB}}","password":"SecurePassword123"}

> {% client.global.set("tokenB", response.body.token); %}

### 13. User B tries to read user A's account -> 404 (not 403: ownership never reveals
###     whether the id exists at all, it just looks identical to "not found")
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenB}}

### 14. User B tries to deposit into user A's account -> 404
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/deposit
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenB}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":10.00}

### 15. User B tries to read user A's transaction history -> 404
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenB}}

### 16. No token at all on a protected endpoint -> 401
GET http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}

### 17. Unknown account id, even with a valid token -> 404
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/000000000000000000000000/deposit
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount":10.00}

### ===== MONEY TRANSFER =====
### IMPORTANT: a transfer runs inside a MongoDB multi-document transaction
### (@Transactional + MongoTransactionManager, see MongoTransactionConfig.java).
### Multi-document transactions require a REPLICA SET - they do NOT work against a
### single standalone mongod. The real atomicity/rollback guarantee (if the deposit
### step were to fail, the earlier withdraw must be undone) can only be observed when
### this app is connected to MongoDB Atlas (a replica set by default) or to a local
### MongoDB that was itself started with a replica set. Against a plain standalone
### local mongod, these requests may fail outright with a transaction-not-supported
### error - that is expected and is a MongoDB limitation, not an application bug.

### 18. User B needs their own account to receive money
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenB}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"accountType":"SAVINGS"}

> {% client.global.set("accountIdB", response.body.id); %}

### 19. User A transfers 200.00 to user B -> 201, senderBalanceAfter 500.00
###     (account A had 700.00 after request #6/#7/#8 above: 700.00 - 200.00 = 500.00)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"toAccountId":"{{accountIdB}}","amount":200.00,"description":"splitting dinner"}

### 20. Self-transfer (same account on both sides) -> 400
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"toAccountId":"{{accountIdA}}","amount":10.00}

### 21. Transfer to a non-existent account -> 404 (the sender's balance must not move)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"toAccountId":"000000000000000000000000","amount":10.00}

### 22. Transfer more than the sender's balance -> 422 Insufficient funds
POST http://localhost:8080/api/accounts/{{accountIdA}}/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{tokenA}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"toAccountId":"{{accountIdB}}","amount":999999.00}